window.docContents[201].resolve({"/Incompressible-Fluid-Mechanics/Euler___-Symmetry___-and-Boussinesq-Flow/#PDEAtlas--Incompressible-Fluid-Mechanics--Euler___-Symmetry___-and-Boussinesq-Flow--Incompressible-Euler-Flow--Initial-value-and-classical-solutions":{"contents":"Initial-value data consist of a force , a prescribed velocity , and\ndistributional incompressibility of . A finite-time residual solution\nstores , , a positive horizon , the Euler momentum equation and\npointwise incompressibility on , and the exact value\n.A residual solution carries no differentiability. A smooth solution also\nrequires ambient smoothness at every positive-time\npoint and one-sided continuity of velocity at . A global smooth solution\nhas these properties at all positive times and relative smoothness on\n. Every global smooth solution restricts, with\nthe same fields and data, to every positive finite horizon. Smoothness alone\non the whole space does not imply the  bounds, decay, and spacetime\nintegrability required by a weak solution, so a smooth solution is weak only\nonce those bounds are proved.\n\nFor zero initial velocity and zero forcing,  and  give a smooth\nEuler solution globally and on every positive finite horizon.\n\nEvery time derivative, convective term, pressure gradient, and divergence is\nzero. Constant zero fields are smooth on the closed physical half-space, and\ntheir initial value is the prescribed zero datum. Restricting the global\nsolution gives the finite-time result.\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Incompressible Fluid Mechanics\u0009Euler, Symmetry, and Boussinesq Flow\u0009Incompressible Euler Flow","header":"8.4.1.1. Initial-value and classical solutions","id":"/Incompressible-Fluid-Mechanics/Euler___-Symmetry___-and-Boussinesq-Flow/#PDEAtlas--Incompressible-Fluid-Mechanics--Euler___-Symmetry___-and-Boussinesq-Flow--Incompressible-Euler-Flow--Initial-value-and-classical-solutions"}});